<PolicyLayout
  title="Consent, Release & Hold Harmless Agreement"
  description="STEM+C participant consent, release of liability, hold harmless, and likeness authorization for robotics program participants and their families."
  effective="the 2026–2027 season, pending legal review"
  notice="This is a reconstruction of the prior agreement, modernized for the current program. It has NOT been reviewed by an attorney and must not be circulated for signature until it has been. Three known defects from the prior version are corrected here and flagged in the review notes at the bottom of this page."
>
  <p>
    This Consent, Release, Hold Harmless and Authorization to Reproduce Physical Likeness (this
    &ldquo;Agreement&rdquo;) is entered into between the Participant named at signature and
    <strong>STEM+C</strong>, an Arizona nonprofit corporation, EIN 83-3523903, together with its
    officers, directors, employees, coaches, mentors, and volunteers.
  </p>

  <h2>Definitions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>&ldquo;Participant&rdquo;</strong> means any individual involved in a STEM+C Event,
    including a student, mentor, volunteer, or other participant, and — where the individual is
    under 18 years of age — that individual's parent or legal guardian signing on their behalf.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>&ldquo;Event&rdquo;</strong> means any STEM+C activity, including team meetings, shop
    work sessions, build sessions, outreach and demonstration events, fundraising activities, league
    play, and travel to and from robotics competitions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>&ldquo;Designees&rdquo;</strong> means those persons and entities — including third
    parties not affiliated with STEM+C — sponsoring, hosting, or publishing an Event, and
    individuals or third parties working with STEM+C in print or video media.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Assumption of risk</h2>
  <p>
    Participant acknowledges that there are risks associated with attending and participating in an
    Event, including without limitation risks inherent in:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the construction, testing, and operation of robots, including robots weighing up to 125 pounds;</li>
    <li>the use of hand tools, power tools, machine tools, and sharp objects;</li>
    <li>working with electrical connections, batteries, and control systems;</li>
    <li>welding, cutting, grinding, 3D printing, and other fabrication processes;</li>
    <li>travel to and from Events, including multi-day travel to regional and championship competitions;</li>
    <li>participation in public competitions and demonstrations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These risks include the risk of injury — including, without limitation, serious bodily harm and
    death — and of property damage. Being fully cognizant of the risks of participating in any such
    Event, Participant knowingly and willingly assumes those risks.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Release and hold harmless</h2>
  <p>
    To the fullest extent permitted by applicable law, Participant releases STEM+C and each of its
    respective officers, directors, employees, coaches, mentors, volunteers, successors, assigns,
    and affiliates from any and all claims, demands, liabilities, damages, costs, and expenses
    (including attorneys' fees and other professional fees and expenses) arising out of, relating
    to, or connected with Participant's participation in any Event.
  </p>
  <p>
    Participant further agrees to indemnify and hold harmless STEM+C and those same persons and
    entities against any and all claims resulting from Participant's participation in any Event.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Medical authorization</h2>
  <p>
    In the event Participant sustains an injury or illness while attending or participating in an
    Event, Participant authorizes STEM+C to administer, or cause to be administered, such first aid
    or other treatment and such medications as reasonably necessary under the circumstances,
    including without limitation treatment by a physician or hospital of STEM+C's choice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Participant is responsible for disclosing to STEM+C any allergy, medical condition, or
    medication that coaches and mentors should know about in an emergency, and for keeping that
    information current.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Likeness and media authorization</h2>
  <p>
    Participant understands that photographs, video recordings, and other recordings will be made of
    participants at Events, including of Participant. Participant consents to those recordings and
    to their use (i) as part of the record of the Event and (ii) in promotion of STEM+C, its teams,
    and other Events.
  </p>
  <p>
    Participant acknowledges that any works made by or for STEM+C (the &ldquo;Works&rdquo;) are
    works made for hire under applicable copyright law, and to the extent Participant has or may
    claim any rights in or to such Works, Participant grants STEM+C all such rights, including
    without limitation the right to use or sub-license them in any and all media now known or later
    developed, in all forms and for all purposes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Participant also grants the same permissions to STEM+C's Designees for the purposes described
    above.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Opting out:</strong> a family that prefers their student not appear in promotional media
    may say so in writing at <a href="mailto:info@stemplusc.org">info@stemplusc.org</a>, and we will
    honor it. Note that STEM+C cannot control recordings made by <em>FIRST</em>, event hosts, other
    teams, or spectators at public competitions.
  </p>

  <h2>5. General</h2>
  <p>
    This Agreement is governed by the laws of the State of Arizona. If any provision is held
    unenforceable, the remaining provisions remain in full force and effect. This Agreement is
    binding on Participant's heirs, executors, administrators, and assigns.
  </p>
  <p>
    This Agreement is separate from, and in addition to, any consent or release that
    <em>FIRST</em> requires directly through its own registration system, and from the
    <a href="/policies/team-agreement">STEM+C Student &amp; Family Team Agreement</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Signature</h2>
  <p>
    Signed by the Participant, and — for any Participant under 18 years of age — by a parent or
    legal guardian, during team registration.
  </p>

  <h2>Notes for legal review</h2>
  <p>
    This section is for the reviewing attorney and should be removed before the document is
    circulated for signature.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Entity name corrected.</strong> The prior version named &ldquo;STEM+C LLC.&rdquo; The
      operating entity is an Arizona nonprofit corporation holding 501(c)(3) status under EIN
      83-3523903. <strong>Please confirm the exact registered corporate name</strong> and conform
      every reference in this document to it — a release naming an entity that does not operate the
      program may not protect the entity that does.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>&ldquo;Aeronautics&rdquo; removed.</strong> The prior version covered &ldquo;Robotics
      or Aeronautics&rdquo; events. Aeronautics is no longer a STEM+C program.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Risk enumeration expanded</strong> to reflect the move from FTC to FRC: heavier
      robots, a wider range of shop tools and fabrication processes, and multi-day competition
      travel.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Please advise on enforceability against minors.</strong> Arizona law limits the
      effect of a parent's pre-injury release of a minor's own claims. The current language may
      need a separate parental-indemnity construction to do what it is intended to do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Please advise on the shop location.</strong> Team meetings and build sessions occur at
      a private residence. Confirm whether that requires additional premises-liability language and
      whether the organization's insurance responds there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Please advise on electronic signature.</strong> We intend to collect signatures
      electronically with an audit trail rather than on paper.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Source note.</strong> The prior agreement exists only as a scanned signature copy; this
      text was reconstructed from it and is not a verbatim reproduction.
    </li>
  </ol>
</PolicyLayout>
